{
  "image_name": "4af87c82863c3e263d86a4d8a672df32.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes systemic iron regulation centered on hepcidin, a liver-derived hormone. The liver integrates signals from body iron levels and inflammation, as well as erythroid demand for iron, to control hepcidin synthesis. Hepcidin then acts on ferroportin, the iron export channel, at two major sites: the duodenum and macrophages.\n\nIn the intestine, dietary iron absorption contributes only about 1 mg per day to the blood. In contrast, macrophages recycle far more iron—about 25 mg per day—after taking up senescent red blood cells. Export of iron from both enterocytes and macrophages occurs through ferroportin, and this iron enters the bloodstream bound to transferrin.\n\nThe circulating transferrin-bound iron is delivered mainly to the bone marrow for erythropoiesis, supporting new red blood cell production, and also to other tissues such as muscle, brain, and the immune system. Red blood cells eventually age and are removed by macrophages, closing the recycling loop.\n\nOverall, the diagram emphasizes that iron homeostasis depends much more on efficient recycling than on dietary uptake, and that hepcidin controls this balance by regulating ferroportin-mediated iron release into the blood.",
